Barnaby was not your average house cat.

While his siblings—three plump, lazy calicos who spent their days draped over the radiator like melted butter—were content to nap in sunbeams and chase the red dot of a laser pointer, Barnaby's emerald eyes were always fixed on the window. He would sit for hours on the windowsill, his chin resting on his paws, watching the world beyond the glass with an intensity that bordered on obsession. He watched the sparrows argue over breadcrumbs. He watched the wind bend the tall grass in the neighbor's yard. He watched the moon rise, fat and golden, and he felt something stir in his chest that no amount of kibble or chin scratches could satisfy.

He was a ginger tabby with a white chest, a white muzzle, and a tail that ended in a perfect white tip, like a candle flame. His fur was thick and warm, the color of autumn leaves, and his paws were soft as velvet. To the humans, he was a beloved pet, a creature of comfort, a cat who purred when you scratched behind his ears and meowed for breakfast at precisely six-fifteen each morning. But Barnaby knew a secret that no one else in the house understood: he was not made for the indoors. He was made for the wild.

The house was a beautiful prison. The kitchen tiles were cool and smooth under his paws, the living room rug was soft and warm, and the windowsills were perfect perches from which to observe the world. But they were walls. They were boundaries. And Barnaby, for all his domestic comfort, was a creature of the outside.

Every night, he dreamed of running. Not the short, frantic dashes he took when the vacuum cleaner roared to life, but long, endless runs through fields of tall grass, through forests of towering pines, along the edges of rivers that glittered like silver ribbons under the moon. In his dreams, he was not Barnaby the house cat. He was something older, something wilder. He was a hunter, a wanderer, a creature of the night.

And then, one crisp October evening, the world changed.

It began with a sound. A soft, metallic creak that came from the back of the house. Barnaby's ears swiveled like satellite dishes, rotating toward the sound. His pupils dilated, swallowing the green of his irises until his eyes were almost entirely black. He lifted his head from the windowsill, where he had been watching the last of the sunset bleed orange and purple across the sky, and he listened.

The back door. It was opening.

He could hear the hinges groaning, the faint whisper of the latch giving way. He could smell the change in the air—the damp, earthy scent of the garden rushing in to replace the familiar smells of carpet cleaner and leftover tuna. His whiskers twitched. His tail, which had been lying limp and relaxed, began to sway with a slow, deliberate rhythm.

He stood up, stretched his long body until his spine arched like a bow, and padded silently across the kitchen floor. The linoleum was cold against his paw pads, and he could feel the vibration of the refrigerator humming beneath him. He passed the water bowl, the food bowl, the little bed by the radiator that the humans had bought him with such love and care. He did not look at any of it. His eyes were fixed on the gap in the door, a sliver of darkness that led to the garden, and beyond the garden, to the world.

He slipped through.

